# WorkflowHub

Downloads Galaxy workflows from WorkflowHub, finds which tool connects to which and translates the tools to bio.tools IDs to compare with the literature graph.

The scripts to run:
```
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/download_workflowhub.py     # Downloads .ga files from WorkflowHub
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/galaxy_tool_pairs.py        # Extracts the .ga workflow nodes and edges
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/map_galaxy_tools.py         # Translates/maps the .ga node names to bio.tools IDs
```

`download_workflowhub.py` gets the metadata of every workflow on WorkflowHub, then downloads the RO-Crate (zip) of each Galaxy workflow and takes out its `.ga` file. Files already downloaded are skipped, so it can be stopped and run again. Add `--limit 5` to test on a few (the summary then goes to `workflows_limit.csv`, so the full `workflows.csv` is not replaced).

`galaxy_tool_pairs.py` extracts the edges from the workflows. Tools are named by their ToolShed repository (e.g. "megahit"), not by bio.tools ID.
Steps that are not real tools, such as inputs and helper tools (`HELPERS` in the script), are skipped, and the "real" tools on each side are connected through them.
Did spot check n = 5 workflows, comparing them to the visual graph in WorkflowHub (e.g. https://workflowhub.org/workflows/4?version=1 and edges in `data/galaxy/galaxy_pairs.csv` with workflow_id == 4).
It produces three files:
- `data/galaxy/galaxy_pairs.csv`  one row per workflow and tool pair: tool A feeds tool B
- `data/galaxy/galaxy_edges.csv`  one row per tool pair, summed over workflows
- `data/galaxy/galaxy_tools.csv`  one row per tool, and how many workflows use it

*Note: Each tool pair is counted once per workflow, so if the same pair appears on several branches of one workflow, it is listed once.*
*We might want TODO something to address that later.*
*Note: Pairs that cross a subworkflow boundary are missed.*

`map_galaxy_tools.py` maps Galaxy tool names to bio.tools IDs. First it uses the Galaxy Codex, which has to be downloaded once:
```
curl -L -o directed-graph/workflowhub/data/mapping/galaxy_codex_tools.tsv https://raw.githubusercontent.com/galaxyproject/galaxy_codex/main/communities/all/resources/tools.tsv
```
If the tool is not in the Codex, it tries to match the name to a bio.tools ID (from `directed-graph/biotools/data/biotools_raw_*.jsonl`). Third, if no match is found, it tries the part of the name before the first `_` (Galaxy often splits one package into several tools, like `samtools_view` -> `samtools`).
Pairs where both tools end up with the same bio.tools ID (e.g. `samtools_view` -> `samtools_sort`) are dropped.
It produces four files:
- `data/mapping/galaxy_tool_map.csv`  the translation map, with a "method" column to tell which matches we trust and which not
- `data/biotools/biotools_pairs.csv`  one row per workflow and tool pair
- `data/biotools/biotools_edges.csv`  one row per pair, summed over workflows
- `data/biotools/biotools_nodes.csv`  one row per bio.tools ID, and how many workflows use it

*Note: The prefix method might be a bit weak, but printing the most common prefix matches suggests it works ok. I added a blocklist for some false matches.*

This is the full data structure

```
data/
├── download/                   download_workflowhub.py (and download_workflowhub_nextflow.py)
│   ├── index.json              all workflow IDs
│   ├── meta/<id>.json          metadata per workflow (all types)
│   ├── crates/                 RO-Crate zips (Galaxy and Nextflow)
│   ├── ga/<id>_v<version>/     .ga files from the crates
│   ├── workflows.csv           one row per workflow
│   └── nextflow/               unpacked Nextflow pipelines <id>_v<version>/, nextflow_workflows.csv
├── galaxy/                     galaxy_tool_pairs.py, ToolShed names
│   ├── galaxy_pairs.csv        tool A feeds tool B, per workflow
│   ├── galaxy_edges.csv        same pairs, counted over workflows
│   └── galaxy_tools.csv        tools and how many workflows use them
├── nextflow/                   extract_nextflow_dags.py: <id>_v<version>/{nodes,edges}.csv, nextflow_dags.csv
├── mapping/                    ToolShed name -> bio.tools ID
│   ├── galaxy_codex_tools.tsv  Galaxy Codex table (downloaded)
│   ├── galaxy_tool_map.csv     the mapping, with method used
│   └── nextflow/               map_nextflow_tools.py: nextflow_tool_map.tsv, nextflow_to_curate.tsv
└── biotools/                   map_galaxy_tools.py, bio.tools IDs
    ├── biotools_pairs.csv      per workflow
    ├── biotools_edges.csv      edges, to compare with the literature graph
    ├── biotools_nodes.csv      node attributes (ToolShed names, n_workflows)
    └── nextflow/               map_nextflow_tools.py: the same three files, and workflows/<id>_v<version>.csv
```

Main output is `data/biotools/biotools_edges.csv`, which has the number of workflows each tool pair appears in and can be used to compare with the literature graph.
Each tool's mapping method is noted in `galaxy_tool_map.csv`. The data should probably be grouped by confidence in match:
codex > name match > prefix name match.
An edge is as reliable as its weaker end, so the comparison can be run on codex-only, codex + name, and all edges to check if weaker mappings change the result.
## Nextflow

```
python3 directed-graph/workflowhub/download_workflowhub_nextflow.py   # Nextflow crates (--ignore-nfcore to skip the nf-core space)
python3 directed-graph/workflowhub/extract_nextflow_dags.py           # DAG of each pipeline, one node per tool
python3 directed-graph/workflowhub/map_nextflow_tools.py              # tool nodes -> bio.tools IDs
```

`download_workflowhub_nextflow.py` downloads every Nextflow workflow (metadata and crates are shared with the Galaxy download) and unpacks it to `data/download/nextflow/<id>_v<version>/`. Incomplete crates (only a link to a git repository, empty or HTML `.nf` files, included files missing) are filled from a shallow `git clone` of the registered repository (needs git; `--no-clone` skips it). Each crate is tried once; `.cloned_from` records the result.

`extract_nextflow_dags.py` (code in `nfdag/`) reads the `.nf` files without running them, for DSL1 and DSL2, with every if/else branch (the union over all parameter choices). Each process is split into the tools it runs, in script order: `bwa-mem2 | samtools` becomes `…/bwa-mem2 → …/samtools` (`kind=chain`); edges between processes (`kind=dag`) leave the last tool and reach the first. The tools of a process are its executables, restricted to the software the module declares (meta.yml, conda, biocontainers); a process that only runs its own scripts keeps one node named after the process. Tools in `nextflow_excluded_tools.txt` (compression, language runtimes, ...; globs like `r-*` allowed) never become nodes and do not cut the flow. `nodes.csv` has all information found per tool node (executables, own scripts, containers, conda packages, versions.yml tools, nf-core meta.yml with bio.tools IDs); `warnings.log` what could not be resolved.

`map_nextflow_tools.py` maps each tool node to bio.tools, first match wins: curated > meta (nf-core meta.yml) > codex (Galaxy Codex: bioconda package or suite) > name > prefix (only for processes without a tool). It reuses the helpers of `map_galaxy_tools.py`. Unmapped tools are left out with their edges, like Galaxy tools without an ID (this leaves a gap, `lost_edges` counts it).

Curation: `data/mapping/nextflow/nextflow_to_curate.tsv` lists the unmapped tools, prefix matches and codex/name disagreements, most used first. Its first columns are those of `nextflow_curated_map.tsv` (tracked, next to the scripts): fill in `action` (`map` with `biotoolsID`, several separated by `;` for a chain, or `drop` = not a bio.tools tool, its neighbours are connected) and copy the row over, then run `map_nextflow_tools.py` again.

Lint and type check (settings in `pyproject.toml`), from this folder: `ruff check . && ruff format --check nfdag && ty check`
